\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Example}
\title{Return and advantage on a three-step episode}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{reinforcement-learning}
\tag{credit-assignment}

\p{On a three-step episode, a discounted return and a baseline-relative
advantage can be computed separately.}

\tikzfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  state/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, align=center, font=\small,
    text width=18mm, minimum height=9mm},
  flow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]}, thick}
]
\node[state] (s0) at (0,0) {$s_0$};
\node[state] (s1) at (2.7,0) {$s_1$};
\node[state] (s2) at (5.4,0) {$s_2$};
\node[state] (s3) at (8.1,0) {terminal};
\draw[flow] (s0) -- node[font=\scriptsize, fill=white] {$r_1=0$} (s1);
\draw[flow] (s1) -- node[font=\scriptsize, fill=white] {$r_2=1$} (s2);
\draw[flow] (s2) -- node[font=\scriptsize, fill=white] {$r_3=2$} (s3);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{Take discount #{\gamma=1/2}. The return attached to the first action is
##{
  G_0=r_1+\gamma r_2+\gamma^2 r_3
     =0+\tfrac12+\tfrac14\cdot2=1.
}
If the declared baseline is #{V(s_0)=0.4}, then the corresponding advantage
estimate is #{\widehat A_0=G_0-V(s_0)=0.6}.}

\p{The calculation instantiates the return and advantage in
\ref{ftip-0030} and \ref{ftip-0032}. It illustrates one baseline choice.
Low variance and correct attribution of terminal reward to an internal
decision require additional assumptions or evidence.}
